+## Links: how `sldb stores add` records the path
+
+`sldb stores add <path>` records the link in the store index (and `stores declare` copies it
+into `links`) in one of two forms, chosen from the text as it was typed, not from the
+resolved path:
+
+- **As written**, when it starts with `/` (absolute), `~` (home, `~user` included) or `$`
+  (an environment variable, `$VAR` or `${VAR}`): `/srv/stores/deskops/.sldb`,
+  `~/stores/deskops/.sldb`, `$SLDB_ROOT/deskops/.sldb`. Clones and worktrees at different
+  depths then resolve the same link. A literal `$` in a folder name is read as a variable.
+- **Relative to the project root** otherwise (`../deskops/.sldb`), so the link survives a
+  checkout of both repositories at another location, as long as they keep their relative place.
+
+The path is checked with `~` and variables expanded, and reading a link
+(`sldb.store.query._linked_stores`) expands them again before resolving it, so the variable
+must be set wherever the store is read. `stores remove` and `stores add --replace` work by
+name and accept any of the forms. From the API, `link_store(..., raw=<text>)` chooses the
+same way; without `raw` the link is recorded relative to the root.
+
